        ["Measuring the latency of a keyboard press means you are measuring:", "How hard you pressed it", "How long you held it down", "The time it takes to begin the press", "How many times you pressed it", 2, "Latency is strictly the measure of delay or wait time before a behavior officially begins."],
        ["If you measure how long a student spends studying, you are measuring:", "Speed", "Duration", "Latency", "Rate of response", 1, "Duration tracks the total continuous length of time a behavior is performed."],
        ["The total time from the start to the finish of a complete episode of behavior is:", "Latency", "Speed", "Duration", "Interval", 1, "Speed measures the total time required to complete an entire episode of behavior from start to finish."],
        ["Measuring whether a behavior occurs within a series of discontinuous intervals is:", "Time Sample Recording", "Interval Recording", "Duration Recording", "Topography", 0, "Time Sample Recording checks for a behavior during brief, separated (discontinuous) intervals to save the researcher time."],
        ["The exact physical form of a behavior (e.g., which paw a rat uses) is:", "Intensity", "Latency", "Topography", "Speed", 2, "Topography refers to the exact physical form, style, or shape of how the behavior is executed."],
        ["Descriptive research methods include Naturalistic Observations and:", "Experimental manipulations", "Case Studies", "Independent Variables", "Control groups", 1, "Naturalistic Observation and Case Studies are both descriptive—they observe behavior without manipulating variables."],
        ["Which research method allows for causal statements?", "Case studies", "Naturalistic observation", "Descriptive research", "Experimental research", 3, "Because it strictly isolates and manipulates variables, experimental research is the only way to prove cause-and-effect."],
        ["A major disadvantage of experimental research is:", "It allows causal statements", "Artificial lab environments limit real-world applicability", "Strict control over variables", "Use of animal subjects", 1, "Experimental control creates highly artificial lab conditions that might not perfectly mirror how animals behave in the real world."],
        ["The Central Nervous System consists of:", "Brain and somatic nerves", "Spinal cord and peripheral nerves", "Brain and spinal cord", "Sensory and motor neurons", 2, "The CNS is the main control center, comprising strictly the brain and the spinal cord."],
        ["The branch of psychology dealing with brain function and behavior by examining brain damage is:", "Cognitive psychology", "Neuropsychology", "Phrenology", "Structuralism", 1, "Neuropsychologists study brain-behavior relationships, often by examining human patients with specific brain lesions or damage."],
        ["Karl Lashley searched for the physical memory trace, which he called the:", "Synapse", "Dendrite", "Engram", "Lobe", 2, "Lashley spent his career cutting into rat brains searching for the physical trace of a memory, which he named the 'engram'."],
        ["Lashley's theory that memories operate across the whole brain is called:", "Hebbian learning", "LTP", "Equipotentiality", "Phrenology", 2, "When Lashley couldn't find a single localized engram, he proposed Equipotentiality—that memory is distributed across the whole brain."],
        ["The part of the neuron that receives signals is the:", "Axon", "Soma", "Dendrite", "Myelin", 2, "Dendrites branch out like trees to receive incoming chemical signals from other surrounding neurons."],
        ["The part of the neuron that transmits electrical signals away is the:", "Axon", "Dendrite", "Soma", "Synapse", 0, "The axon is the long tail that transmits the electrical action potential away from the cell body to the next neuron."],
        ["'Neurons that fire together, wire together' describes:", "Equipotentiality", "Hebbian Learning", "Lashley's Engram", "Phrenology", 1, "Donald Hebb proposed that mutual activity automatically strengthens neural connections: 'neurons that fire together, wire together'."],
        ["A narrow 20-nanometer gap between neurons is the:", "Soma", "Synapse", "Axon", "Dendrite", 1, "The synapse is the microscopic gap where neurotransmitters cross from the presynaptic axon to the postsynaptic dendrite."],
        ["When synaptic transmission becomes more effective from recent activity, it is called:", "Long-Term Depression (LTD)", "Neurogenesis", "Equipotentiality", "Long-Term Potentiation (LTP)", 3, "LTP is the physical strengthening of a synapse, making future transmission easier and faster, which forms the basis of learning."],
        ["Glutamate is an example of an:", "Inhibitory neurotransmitter", "Excitatory neurotransmitter", "Neuromodulator", "Engram", 1, "Glutamate is an excitatory neurotransmitter, meaning it increases the likelihood that the receiving neuron will fire."],
        ["GABA is an example of an:", "Inhibitory neurotransmitter", "Excitatory neurotransmitter", "Engram", "Electrode", 0, "GABA reduces the likelihood that the receiving neuron will fire, acting as the brain's primary inhibitory neurotransmitter."],
        ["Researchers use microelectrodes to deliver tiny amounts of current into the brain. This is:", "fMRI", "Electromagnetic stimulation", "Single-cell recording", "Biochemical control", 1, "Researchers can map brain functions by delivering tiny electrical currents through implanted microelectrodes to stimulate specific regions."],
        ["Which lobe is important for processing visual inputs?", "Frontal", "Parietal", "Occipital", "Temporal", 2, "The Occipital lobe, located at the very back of the brain, is the primary processing center for visual information."],
        ["Which lobe enables a person to plan and perform actions?", "Temporal", "Occipital", "Parietal", "Frontal", 3, "The Frontal lobe handles executive functions, high-level planning, decision-making, and initiating motor actions."],
        ["The Primary Motor Cortex (M1) receives high-level plans from the:", "Occipital lobe", "Frontal lobe", "Cerebellum", "Brainstem", 1, "The Frontal lobe makes the high-level plan, and then passes it to the Primary Motor Cortex (M1) to execute the specific muscle movements."],
        ["Which brain structure regulates complex voluntary muscular movement and motor-reflex conditioning?", "Thalamus", "Cerebellum", "Brainstem", "Amygdala", 1, "The Cerebellum controls fine motor coordination and is absolutely essential for the classical conditioning of motor reflexes like blinking."],
        ["Which functional imaging technique tracks local changes in blood oxygen levels?", "EEG", "DTI", "MRI", "fMRI", 3, "fMRI creates a functional, real-time map of brain activity by tracking exactly where oxygen-rich blood is flowing."],
        ["Which imaging technique measures electrical activity via scalp electrodes?", "fMRI", "EEG", "DTI", "MRI", 1, "EEG uses non-invasive scalp electrodes to measure the raw electrical waves produced by brain activity."],
        ["Measuring behavior via the force or magnitude is measuring its:", "Latency", "Intensity", "Duration", "Speed", 1, "Intensity measures the sheer force, volume, or magnitude behind a specific behavior."],
        ["If a behavior is defined as publicly observable, it is:", "Covert", "Overt", "Aversive", "Appetitive", 1, "Overt behaviors are outward actions that can be readily seen, measured, and recorded by an outside observer."],
        ["Dreams and thoughts are examples of:", "Overt behaviors", "Covert behaviors", "Independent variables", "Reflexes", 1, "Covert behaviors happen internally (like private thoughts or dreams) and cannot be directly observed by others."],
        ["The belief that learning facts generalize across many species is the:", "Equipotentiality theory", "Law of Effect", "General Process Theory", "Hebbian law", 2, "General Process Theory assumes basic learning principles (like classical conditioning) are universal and apply across many different species."],
        ["Measuring how many times a behavior occurs in a time period is measuring its:", "Speed", "Duration", "Rate of response", "Latency", 2, "Rate of response calculates frequency over a set time period (e.g., measuring 5 lever presses per minute)."],
        ["A cumulative recorder shows a steeper line when:", "Rate of response is higher", "The animal is sleeping", "Rate of response is lower", "Latency is high", 0, "On a cumulative recorder, every response moves the pen up. A fast response rate moves the pen up quickly, creating a steep slope."],
        ["DTI (Diffusion Tensor Imaging) is a form of:", "Functional neuroimaging", "Structural neuroimaging", "Single-cell recording", "Biochemical control", 1, "DTI (like standard MRI) provides a static picture of brain structures and neural connections, not real-time activity."],
        ["Neuromodulators differ from standard neurotransmitters because they:", "Inhibit firing", "Excite firing", "Affect activity in many neurons rather than a single synapse", "Cannot cross the synapse", 2, "Neuromodulators wash over larger areas of the brain to alter the excitability of many neurons at once, rather than targeting a single synapse."],
        ["Using drugs to alter neurotransmitter release is an example of:", "Electromagnetic stimulation", "Biochemical control", "Single-cell recording", "fMRI", 1, "Drugs provide biochemical control by chemically altering presynaptic neurotransmitter release or postsynaptic receptor binding."],
        ["Which lobe processes somatosensory (touch) inputs?", "Frontal", "Occipital", "Parietal", "Temporal", 2, "The Parietal lobe sits at the top of the brain and processes somatosensory information like touch, pressure, and temperature."],
        ["Which lobe is important for language, auditory processing, and forming new memories?", "Temporal", "Occipital", "Parietal", "Frontal", 0, "The Temporal lobe is located at the sides of the brain and processes sound, language, and is heavily involved in forming new memories."],
        ["The brainstem connects the brain to the spinal cord and regulates:", "High-level planning", "Complex motor conditioning", "Automatic functions like breathing", "Visual processing", 2, "The brainstem handles fundamental, unconscious life-support functions like breathing, heart rate, and body temperature."],
        ["Environmental enrichment for rats leads to visible structural changes in their:", "Fur", "Neurons", "Liver", "Eyes", 1, "Enriched environments physically alter the brain, leading to increased dendritic branching and structurally stronger synapses in neurons."],
        ["Event-Related Potentials (ERPs) are usually averaged from which technique?", "fMRI", "EEG", "MRI", "DTI", 1, "ERPs are created by averaging dozens of raw EEG wave recordings to isolate the brain's specific electrical response to a stimulus."],
        ["Which component integrates signals from the dendrites?", "Axon", "Synapse", "Receptor", "Cell Body (Soma)", 3, "The Soma (Cell Body) collects and integrates all the incoming signals received by the dendrites before deciding to pass the signal to the axon."],
        ["Phrenology assumed cortical abilities were evident from:", "fMRI scans", "Bumps in the skull", "Blood oxygen levels", "Electrical spiking", 1, "Phrenology was an early pseudoscience that falsely claimed personality traits and brain power could be read by feeling the bumps on a person's skull."]
